Z||000007||hugh||01-01-2001||11:09 AM||||Phil,Why would you go down in size?Handling?Cost?Clearance?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||01-01-2001||11:54 AM||||just want to. i was looking at tires and wheels and saw in a catalog the recommended wheel for this tire (255's) is as small as 7" i have 7.5's so i'm in the range (i know some one will say thats too small but thats from two sources and i'm not a racer). you need to switch to coilovers too.<P> plus i got the idea of big tires from the hot rod issue with the superduty fiero (oct. 87) . they got 315/35-17's to fit by switching to coil overs and making some adapters to move the struts inboard (wish i could get some thing like that). <P>any way, i want to switch down to get closer to the right overall dia. and 245's are as wide as i can go with what i have. for some reason you can get nice sizes for 15's and 17" and up but not 16's. <P>last (probaly more explantion than you wanted), i went crazy over the last year and now have 4 fieros ( making arrangements to pick up #4), and since i like a particular wheel i'm trying for uniformity bettween them so i can swap wheels around if i want. if i stay with 255's they would all need coil overs. 225's are what i have in front and if i use that size all around it frees things up more.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phaeton||01-01-2001||01:36 PM||||I was running 225/50-16's on all four, but dropped back to 215's in the front because my Fiero would'nose' around on straight stretches with the wider fronts.  The Fiero has stock suspension geometry all around and would still take a set and corner like crazyglue was on the tires, but wandered something fierce going down the straights.  Putting the thinner fronts on really made the Fiero track almost hands free again.<P>Not enough knowledge on my part to make the big tires work, sure did look cool though.||||reg||12
Z||000010||Wannabe||01-01-2001||11:38 PM||||255-40-17 on 17x9s will fit and clear everything with the right offset.||||reg||1
Z||000011||mshill||01-02-2001||12:20 AM||||rock, I got the non-stretch version of the F50 since it is my first kit I wanted it to be a little easier than having to deal with a stretch the first time.  Initially, I thought that it might just look like a Fiero with a new skin, but once done, if I put it next to my 86SE you won't even believe it is the same chassis.  It is really cool!||||reg||1
